{"card_hash": "6da9771a3dd242ac6fb8f94ec2d1c6a6bdd0737638336d64a8c793300bc49431", "channel": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "chars": 35335, "chars_all_clips": 35335, "chars_chosen_clips": 1440, "clip_count": 59, "clips": [{"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=leXa7EKUPFk\u0026t=400s", "end": 449.57, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "5f8cb2f8bc804b2acb177501e42a1af4edf71a399dc2bf1f6ebc0e527c547f8e", "start": 400.23, "text": "Have you ever written a program that's answered questions about its own behavior? Probably not. Would you like to learn how to do that? So by the end of the hour, you'll be able to write this program and many more like it that know how to a", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}, {"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=leXa7EKUPFk\u0026t=808s", "end": 857.31, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "73cf2f44edb0e1c143a6bab1c91395231323dd84ade7a38f0d911b7c695801df", "start": 808.71, "text": "How does it answer a how question? Sebastian? SEBASTIAN: It goes down one level. PROFESSOR PATRICK WINSTON: You go down one level. So you start off all the way up here with a put-on. You will say, oh, well I did these four things. You say, ", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}, {"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=leXa7EKUPFk\u0026t=952s", "end": 1002.8, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "f715005f75f290685bfb5c357e856f1cef9c10a788efb5b3afc669e974c995b9", "start": 952.55, "text": "For example, I could say, why did you put B6 on B3? Because you told me to. All right, so the complexity of the behavior is largely a consequence not of the complexity of the program in this particular case, but the building of this giant g", "timing": "source_cues", "truncated": true}, {"deep_link": "https://youtube.com/watch?v=leXa7EKUPFk\u0026t=1570s", "end": 1630.42, "evidence_kind": "timed_clip", "excerpt_id": "50e0b2c7699466d0330a0356f44d0206916c69d5f3b0b42f2a0ad1c087782fce", "start": 1570.44, "text": "So look at this. If I say to